{"name":"Argos","hex":"#bdbdb7","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Argos","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A city in the Peloponnese, Greece.","The dog of Odysseus in Homer's Odyssey."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Argos"}],"entries":[{"title":"Argos, Peloponnese","blocks":[{"text":"Argos is a city and a former municipality in the Peloponnese region of Greece, and one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world. It is the largest city in Argolis, having nearly twice the population of its capital, Nafplio."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/The_city_of_Argos_from_the_Castle_of_Larissa_on_September_5%2C_2020.jpg/330px-The_city_of_Argos_from_the_Castle_of_Larissa_on_September_5%2C_2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argos%2C_Peloponnese"},{"title":"Argos (retailer)","blocks":[{"text":"Argos Limited is a British retailer founded in 1972 by Richard Tompkins. Tompkins established Argos by evolving his existing Green Shield Stamps concept. Argos retained Green Shield Stamps' catalogue-store model, where customers browse from a book rather than from shelves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Argos_logo.svg/330px-Argos_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argos_(retailer)"},{"title":"Argos (dog)","blocks":[{"text":"In Homer's Odyssey, Argos, sometimes referred to as Argus, is Odysseus's faithful dog. Bred to be a hunting dog before Odysseus leaves for the Trojan War, Argos is neglected after Odysseus is presumed dead. Twenty years later, Odysseus returns to Ithaca and finds him lying in piles of manure, immobile from old age and neglect, and infested with parasites. When Argos sees Odysseus, he immediately recognizes him, dropping his ears and wagging his tail. Disguised as a beggar, Odysseus cannot greet his dog without revealing his identity, but secretly weeps. Upon seeing his master return home, Argos dies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Ulysses_and_Argus_-_Frederick_Stacpoole_after_Briton_Rivi%C3%A8re_%28BM_2010%2C7081.6046%29_-_cropped.jpg/330px-Ulysses_and_Argus_-_Frederick_Stacpoole_after_Briton_Rivi%C3%A8re_%28BM_2010%2C7081.6046%29_-_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argos_(dog)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/aa/Argos_Siegel.png/120px-Argos_Siegel.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/aa/Argos_Siegel.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/aa/Argos_Siegel.png/120px-Argos_Siegel.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArgos_Siegel.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Argos%2C_Triobol%2C_c.270-250_BC%2C_HGC_5-670.jpg/500px-Argos%2C_Triobol%2C_c.270-250_BC%2C_HGC_5-670.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Argos%2C_Triobol%2C_c.270-250_BC%2C_HGC_5-670.jpg/500px-Argos%2C_Triobol%2C_c.270-250_BC%2C_HGC_5-670.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Triobol of Argos, struck c. 270–250 BC. Obv.: forepart of a wolf, alluding to Apollo Lykeios, the patron-god of the city; rev.: large A (for Argos) within an incuse square.[9]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArgos%2C_Triobol%2C_c.270-250_BC%2C_HGC_5-670.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Argos_Heraion_Plain.jpg/500px-Argos_Her